🔍 What is Indexing in SQL?  it is also called non cluster index
Indexing is a database optimization technique used to speed up the retrieval of records from a table.

📘 Simple Definition:
Index is like a book index – it helps the database find data faster, without scanning the whole table.

🔧 How It Works:
When you create an index on a column (e.g., name), the database creates a separate structure that holds the values of that column in sorted order, along with pointers to the actual rows.

So when you run a SELECT query with WHERE name = 'John', the database looks in the index first.

🛠️ Example:
sql
Copy
Edit
CREATE INDEX idx_employee_name ON employees(name);
This will create an index on the name column in the employees table.

✅ Benefits of Indexing:
Faster SELECT queries

Improves performance on WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY, and GROUP BY

⚠️ Downsides:
Slows down INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE (because index must also be updated)

Takes extra storage space

Too many indexes can reduce performance

📚 Types of Indexes:
| Type              | Description                                     |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| **Single-column** | Index on one column                             |
| **Composite**     | Index on multiple columns                       |
| **Unique**        | Prevents duplicate values                       |
| **Full-text**     | For searching text fields                       |
| **Clustered**     | Sorts the actual data rows (only one per table) |
| **Non-clustered** | Separate from data, points to rows              |
